#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162852} :activity/id #uuid "91237c92-d7c1-506c-95d7-177c7680008d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048300 :topic/id #uuid "927333d2-5d1a-53f3-be91-292cad443566" :topic/math-academy-id 3122} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162852 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163227 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= x_1^2-8x_1x_2 + x_2^2\n$$\n\n and the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ that defines  the **change of variable**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ \n\n How do we express $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}?$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\ngives the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we substitute these into our quadratic form expression, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})^{2}+8(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}){y}_{2}+(-{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+16{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-8{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}+12{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "129771b6-7bea-5c9d-b4c7-05ef8a7493b2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4885 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b50d538b-7a37-5a5b-af5f-d7d425017761" :step/math-academy-id 30536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162873}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161991 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161783 :question/id #uuid "248c9a29-6698-5c8c-b674-0b0dc537edf0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8177"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f203c9bf-7d12-56db-aafe-6639162eae93" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050407}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357674}] :knowledge-point/title "Changing Variables in a Quadratic Form Given Old Variables Through New Variables"} :step/id #uuid "865b4c88-60a2-55e6-b4b2-f027d0718ebe" :step/math-academy-id 30612 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162874}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161992 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161784 :question/id #uuid "1c199cdf-ca23-53e8-8b5d-1ba43c842d41" :question/math-academy-id "e-8178"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f2a5391-d8bb-5084-941a-bb787e8729f3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378018}] :knowledge-point/title "Changing Variables in a Quadratic Form Given New Variables Through Old Variables"} :step/id #uuid "124e7230-af5a-57e2-95dc-9c539b7d58db" :step/math-academy-id 30613 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162875}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163236 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n \n\nand an invertible matrix $P$ that defines  the change of variable\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIf this change of variable is applied to the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ what will be the new matrix of $Q$?\n\nBy making the necessary substitutions, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}{P}^{T}\\,AP\\,y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}AP)y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the new matrix of our quadratic form is \n\n$$\nB=  P^T \\! A P.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "61f41743-ca11-557a-b0c1-bba72921428a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4901 :tutorial/title "The Connection Between Matrices of Quadratic Forms in Different Bases"} :step/id #uuid "5900140f-95ae-5f3f-85f7-2351c6ab39d0" :step/math-academy-id 30614 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162876}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162876 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161993 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161785 :question/id #uuid "4c239ba2-66c9-5170-8a5a-9f7071315dd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8179"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "331cb610-6168-5161-b821-237c713dedaa" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394929}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form Given a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "d3a1f8da-11aa-5561-b435-f00a5790a026" :step/math-academy-id 30615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162877}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163237 :tutorial/content "Two quadratic forms $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ on $\\Bbb R^n$ are said to be **equivalent** if they can be obtained from each other through a change of variable.\n\n> *The two quadratic forms with matrices $A$ and $B$ are equivalent if and only if there exists an invertible matrix $P$ such that* \n> \n> $$\n> B = P^T \\! A \\, P.\n> $$\n\n**Note:** Equivalent quadratic forms represent the same function from  $\\Bbb R^n$ to $\\Bbb R.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "abe7e02c-7997-5e41-9c51-98c6291198a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4902 :tutorial/title "Equivalent Quadratic Forms"} :step/id #uuid "75121285-4880-5444-b03b-36d42d881cfe" :step/math-academy-id 30616}] :activity/title "Change of Variables in Quadratic Forms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162218}]}
